  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nTrough that dropped south out of central Canada currently\nbecoming more closed off with tightening pressure gradients and\nwill push northeast today across the Ohio River valley and into\nthe northeastern states through Friday. As it does gusty\neasterly to northeasterly winds with gusts of 25 to 35 mph \nespecially for the eastern third of the CWA will develop later \nthis afternoon into tonight. A Gale Warning for southern Lake \nMichigan has been issued for this evening and tonight to address\nthese gusty winds over the lake. Advected moisture from the \nsoutheast and Atlantic will also stream northwestward into the \nregion today with WAA aloft and isentropic lifting on the \nnortheastern quadrant of the low pressure system which will \nbring a steady rainfall across the area later today. Surface\nhighs though look to remain in the mid 40s. The rainfall will \nspread from the south and push northward across the area into \nthis afternoon. A pretty steady plume of moisture aloft and \nsubsequent rainfall will continue today and through Friday \nafternoon. Overnight tonight some CAA begins to wrap \nsouthwestward into the area with the northwesterly portions of \nthe system over the area and will allow for some snow showers to\nmix in with the rainfall. At this time no real snowfall \naccumulations/impacts are expected. Latest rainfall totals\nexpected have increased a bit ranging from about 0.75\" to 1.25\"\nwith the higher amounts for the southeastern portions of the\narea.\n\nAs the system pushes northeastward winds will begin to diminish\ntonight and the steady rain will look to become more scattered\nby Friday afternoon as the storm pulls further away from the\nregion. A more zonal flow develops over the region and high\ntemperatures will generally remain in the mid to upper 40s\nthrough Tuesday. Saturday a break in precipitation with a\ndecrease in clouds as a ridge axis pushes through will allow a\nfew locations to approach the low 50s. Clouds though look to\nincrease again by Sunday with another trough albeit weaker than\nthe first system pushing eastward from the Plains/Midwest \nstates across the region which will bring another shot of light\nrain to the area. With colder overnight lows by Sunday night\ninto early Monday morning a few light snow showers as the\nprecipitation begins to dissipate will be possible. \n\nZonal flow continues into next week with mainly dry conditions\nand a gradual increase in synoptic heights which will allow \ntemperatures to warm with highs by Wednesday and Thursday\ngetting back into the 50s and a few locales flirting with highs\nnear 60. By late Wednesday another disturbance arrives with\nanother chance for some light rainfall.",
